WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.910
Modern matrices can hold billions of entries, yet
they're often nearly low rank.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.312
This Stanford work introduces LPLR,

00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:13.528
a randomized algorithm that factorizes any matrix
into two low-rank factors,

00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:16.067
then quantizes them to low precision,

00:00:16.147 --> 00:00:21.625
compressing to one bit per coordinate while
matching prior compression on images,

00:00:21.705 --> 00:00:23.890
embeddings, and LlaMa-7b weights.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.902
Matrices are everywhere in science and machine learning,

00:00:27.982 --> 00:00:32.741
but modern ones hold billions of elements that
strain memory and compute.

00:00:32.821 --> 00:00:35.458
Luckily, many are approximately low rank.

00:00:35.538 --> 00:00:40.960
Prior work exploited either low-rank structure or
low-precision quantization alone;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.130
combining both in one factorization, with provable
error control, stayed open.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.705
If a matrix is low rank, we can write it as a tall
factor times a wide one,

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:54.940
and storing those in low precision saves more.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.512
But doing this crudely at low bit budgets degrades
accuracy fast,

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:04.435
and the exact SVD route costs order n d squared,
prohibitive at scale.

00:01:04.515 --> 00:01:09.710
It seeks a randomized way to get both factors with
small, analyzable error.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.138
The core contribution is LPLR,

00:01:12.218 --> 00:01:18.735
Low-Precision Low-Rank factorization: a randomized
algorithm that exploits low-rank structure

00:01:18.815 --> 00:01:21.289
and quantizes the resulting factors.

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:28.880
The authors derive rigorous upper bounds on the
approximation error in terms of target rank and bit budget,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.356
exposing a tunable compression-accuracy tradeoff.

00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:36.896
They add an SVD-based variant, LPLR-SVD, and
validate on images,

00:01:36.976 --> 00:01:40.230
embedding classification, and LlaMa-7b weights.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.315
LPLR runs in a few steps.

00:01:42.395 --> 00:01:47.312
It draws a Gaussian sketching matrix S and forms
the sketch A times S,

00:01:47.392 --> 00:01:50.952
a randomized rangefinder for the column space of A,

00:01:51.032 --> 00:01:53.380
then quantizes it to get Q of A S.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.305
Next it least-squares-projects A's columns onto
this quantized basis,

00:01:58.385 --> 00:02:02.660
solving for the projection W star, then quantizes W
star too,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.516
returning factors L and R.

00:02:04.596 --> 00:02:11.797
The key insight: Gaussian sketches are
Johnson-Lindenstrauss embeddings with an equalization property,

00:02:11.877 --> 00:02:16.937
so reconstruction error stays order one and doesn't
grow with dimension,

00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:19.650
keeping LPLR accurate even at one bit.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.184
Experiments span a deliberately diverse set of matrices.

00:02:24.264 --> 00:02:30.612
For images: the Shepp-Logan phantom, a Hubble image
of Jupiter, and an MR brain scan.

00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:34.846
For embeddings: CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 from MobileNetV3,

00:02:34.926 --> 00:02:41.122
and IMDB and Emotion text from BERT, scored by a
three-nearest-neighbor classifier.

00:02:41.202 --> 00:02:44.601
And for LLMs, the weight matrices of LlaMa-7b.

00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:50.650
Every method gets the same total bit budget,
reporting relative Frobenius error.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:56.552
The headline: LPLR delivers extreme compression,
near one bit per coordinate,

00:02:56.632 --> 00:02:58.662
while preserving performance.

00:02:58.742 --> 00:03:04.410
On CIFAR-10 embeddings at a single bit, it retains
ninety-two percent accuracy,

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:10.376
matching the unquantized ninety-one, while naive
quantization collapses to eleven.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:14.159
On CIFAR-100, seventy-nine percent versus about one.

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:15.614
On LlaMa-7b weights,

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:20.925
LPLR-SVD reaches mean relative Frobenius error near
zero point five four,

00:03:21.005 --> 00:03:25.800
against zero point eight four for naive quant, with
lower variance.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.302
The paper separates two effects: low-rank structure
and quantization precision.

00:03:31.382 --> 00:03:35.188
Table 3 sweeps the bit-budget triplet at fixed
compression,

00:03:35.268 --> 00:03:41.049
showing that shifting budget toward the
non-quantized reference steadily lowers Frobenius

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:43.353
error for LPLR and its SVD variant.

00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:44.934
Across embedding tables,

00:03:45.014 --> 00:03:51.651
raising the quantization budget from one to four
bits moves LPLR from beating baselines toward parity,

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.680
its edge largest where compression is extreme.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.344
By the numbers: LPLR compresses to one bit per coordinate.

00:03:59.424 --> 00:04:03.709
On CIFAR-10 at one bit, ninety-two percent accuracy
versus eleven for naive quant,

00:04:03.789 --> 00:04:08.073
matching the unquantized ninety-one.

00:04:08.153 --> 00:04:12.437
On CIFAR-100, seventy-nine versus one.

00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:16.801
On LlaMa-7b,

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:21.166
mean Frobenius error zero point five four for
LPLR-SVD versus zero point eight four.

00:04:21.246 --> 00:04:25.530
And it runs in order n d m time, far cheaper than a
full SVD.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:29.978
The takeaway: low rank and low precision are
complementary,

00:04:30.058 --> 00:04:32.649
and exploiting them together pays off.

00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:37.992
By sketching a matrix with a Gaussian projection
and quantizing the factors,

00:04:38.072 --> 00:04:43.476
LPLR compresses to one bit per coordinate while
preserving accuracy on images,

00:04:43.556 --> 00:04:47.413
embeddings, and LLM weights, with provable error bounds.

00:04:47.493 --> 00:04:51.490
And by avoiding costly SVD, it scales to largest matrices.
